  "image_name": "6b9716f4f991814653ff37d2d63bbd94.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three ways a liquid-crystal elastomer can be reversibly reconfigured by different stimuli. In each case, elongated molecular units have an initial preferred alignment along the director, n, and then become less aligned or change shape, producing a macroscopic response.\n\nThe top schematic shows a thermotropic system: heating disrupts the initial vertical orientational order, while cooling restores it. The molecular rods go from a more uniformly aligned state to a more disordered one, indicating a temperature-driven phase change.\n\nThe middle schematic shows a phototropic system containing photochromic molecules, noted as an azobenzene-type example. Under ultraviolet light, some elongated units appear to switch to a bent form, which reduces the overall alignment. Visible light reverses this, returning the system toward the original ordered state.\n\nThe bottom schematic illustrates electromechanical actuation in an LCE device with compliant electrodes. Applying voltage causes the thin film to compress through its thickness and expand preferentially in-plane along one direction, changing its shape. Turning the voltage off reverses the deformation. Overall, the figure conveys that thermal, optical, and electrical inputs can all trigger reversible molecular reorganization and large-scale shape change in liquid-crystal elastomer materials.",
